WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.469
-Our next guest
is an acclaimed author,

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:05.071
whose second novel,
"The Women Could Fly,"

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:07.006
is on sale now
wherever you buy your books.

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:09.976
Please welcome to the show
Megan Giddings, everyone.

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:11.644
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:21.654 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.656 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:25.458
Welcome to the show.
-Hi!

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:28.227
-I'm so excited about this,
to have you here.

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:29.895
But I also know
that you were a professor

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:31.698
at University of Minnesota.
-I am.

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:33.365
-So I need to ask,

00:00:33.365 --> 00:00:35.634
did you have to cancel
any classes to be here?

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:39.071
-I did. And my
grad students were like...

00:00:39.071 --> 00:00:40.873
[ Gasping ]
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:43.342
So...
-So they forgave you for --

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:44.644
I mean,
guess they're probably psyched

00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:46.512
to have a day off anyway.
-Oh, they're super psyched.

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:47.947
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:50.083
-Oh, this worked out great for
everybody, all parties involved.

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:52.084
-We're all happy right now.
-All right, good.

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:54.821
You go back tomorrow, and you
have to teach class tomorrow?

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:57.857
-I don't. I have to just
go to departmental meetings.

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:00.160
So, you can keep me for another
day, so I can miss those, too.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.296
-No worries. I will keep you out
of the departmental meetings.

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:06.599
So, you had your first book,
which was critically acclaimed,

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:09.235
came out right at the beginning
of the pandemic.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.103
-Yes.
-And then, I'm wondering,

00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:13.372
did you jump right into writing
the new one,

00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:14.974
or did you give yourself
some time?

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:18.111
-I actually jumped right into
the new one in 2018.

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:21.447
So it's one of those things,
like, you never know

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:23.916
if someone's gonna
buy your book or your art,

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:26.452
so you have to have the next
project to sustain you.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.821
You keep working.
-Right, because, of course,

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:31.858
before "Lakewood" came out,
you had a long lead time,

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:33.392
I'm assuming,
between when you finished it

00:01:33.392 --> 00:01:34.828
and when it came out.
-Right.

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:37.230
-Instead of taking a break,
you just leaped into this one.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.932
-Yeah, especially with your
first one, you never know.

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:41.467
-Right.
-Yeah.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.869
-So you -- this book covers

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:47.106
fantasy, witchcraft,
race, gender.

00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:50.043
It's a lot of different ideas
woven together beautifully.

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.911
Where did the idea
first occur to you

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.914
to take on themes like these?

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:58.685
-I think it's part of, like,
being alive right now

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:01.687
and being a woman, where --

00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:04.390
Because there's a registry
in the book where women,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.458
if you're not married
by the age of 30,

00:02:06.458 --> 00:02:08.995
you have a choice.
You either get married

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:10.763
and kind of suck it up,

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:13.433
or you register with the state
as a witch.

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:15.768
Because how dare a woman
not be married at 30.

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:16.969
-Yeah.
-And some of it

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:18.370
also comes from a joke.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.207
Like, I'm from the Midwest,
and there's kind of that vibe --

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:23.643
"If you're not married by 28,
what's wrong with you?"

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.211
-Uh-huh?
-"Are you a witch?

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.847
Here's a whole book about it."
-There you go.

00:02:27.847 --> 00:02:29.482
Sometimes a good Midwestern joke

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.817
is a great leaping-off point
for literature.

00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:34.354
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:37.022
It does take place
in an alternate reality

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:39.058
but one that, you know,
certainly doesn't seem

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:42.228
too far removed from the one
we're living through now.

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.496
Is it important for you to sort
of separate it a little bit?

00:02:44.496 --> 00:02:46.765
Does that let your fiction
breathe a bit more?

00:02:46.765 --> 00:02:49.001
-Yeah, I get --

00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:52.405
Like, the themes of this book,
it's really hard for me

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.607
to just -- if I flat out
wrote the book in this world,

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:56.208
it would just be a rant.

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:58.478
It would be, "Why do we
treat women like this?

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:01.146
Why aren't --
Why aren't we people?"

00:03:01.146 --> 00:03:02.615
Oh, my God,
I could be asking that every day

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:05.184
the rest of my life -- "Why
aren't women people to people?"

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:06.419
[ Laughter ]
Aah!

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:10.489
And so I wrote this book,
and it gave me the space

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:12.658
to actually try to get into it.

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:14.394
I wanted to think really deeply
about the things

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:17.630
that made me angry,
but I also wanted room

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.399
to, like, play and have fun.

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:21.801
There are parties.
There are witches.

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:24.203
There's joy.
There's jokes.

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:26.805
And I think that space
gave me the room to do that.

00:03:26.805 --> 00:03:29.509
-So did you feel
and do you hope both that

00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:30.843
it was cathartic for you
to write it?

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:32.145
Do you feel like
it'll be cathartic

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:33.179
for people to read, as well?

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:34.447
Is that one of the things
you lay out

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:36.416
when you try to approach this?

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:38.117
-Especially with this book,

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.786
yeah, I wanted people
to feel catharsis.

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:42.688
I want them to feel seen.

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:44.623
In the ending -- I'm trying not
to give away the ending

00:03:44.623 --> 00:03:45.858
'cause my publicist would die

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:46.892
if I gave away
the ending right now.

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:49.128
-Yeah, let's not
give away the ending.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:53.499
-But it's about wanting people
to find community,

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:56.502
to be there for one another.
I wanted there to be hope.

00:03:56.502 --> 00:04:00.039
I can't write a book
about feminism and wanting to be

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:03.675
engaged with other people
if we also couldn't want

00:04:03.675 --> 00:04:06.379
to make something more
and make the world beautiful.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:10.516
-That is a very lovely way
to set out in your writing.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:12.084
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:18.557
Your parents are not writers,
but your father, a storyteller.

00:04:18.557 --> 00:04:21.861
-Yeah, if you ever have
the pleasure of going out

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.863
to dinner with my dad,
oh, highly recommend.

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:26.332
-Yes.
-He loves going out to dinner.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:29.902
But, I mean, whose dad doesn't?
-Whose dad doesn't? Yeah.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:33.339
-But he really likes looking
around and being like --

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:34.940
telling stories about people,

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.809
and he makes up
the weirdest stories.

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:38.444
-He makes them up?
-He makes them up.

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.846
-Okay, gotcha.
-And people who have had dinner

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:42.949
with my dad, like,
they'll see me sometimes

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:45.083
doing the same thing
where I'm like,

00:04:45.083 --> 00:04:46.452
"Oh, those people
are in a fight,

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:47.753
and I bet it's about this."

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:48.954
And then I keep adding,
they're like,

00:04:48.954 --> 00:04:51.256
"You're just like your father."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.256 --> 00:04:53.693
-Well, sometimes I think
just being around --

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:54.827
I was certainly very lucky.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.229
My father
is a storyteller, as well.

00:04:57.229 --> 00:05:00.366
And it's interesting being
around that as a young person.

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:02.435
I do think it just helps you

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.437
understand how structure
is important

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:06.405
and how to hold people's
attention is important.

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:09.541
And, obviously, he had
quite an influence on you

00:05:09.541 --> 00:05:11.210
because you certainly know how
to put it on the page.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.213
-Oh, thank you.
That's intimidatingly nice.

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:15.547
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:17.783
-Well, congratulations
on the book.

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:19.218
And thank you to your students

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:21.387
for loaning you to us
for the day.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.989
Congratulations, Megan.

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:25.991
Megan Giddings, everybody.
"The Women Could Fly"

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:27.827
is available now
wherever you buy your books,

00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:29.595
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:31.397
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

